    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-human-rights-commission-act-1986-article-51",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "51 1.",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-human-rights-commission-act-1986#article-51"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "australian-human-rights-commission-act-1986.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "australian-human-rights-commission-act-1986.pdf, p.150",
      "values:originalText": "Any State Party to the present Covenant may propose an amendment\nand file it with the Secretary-General of the United Nations. The\nSecretary-General of the United Nations shall thereupon communicate any\nproposed amendments to the States Parties to the present Covenant with a\nrequest that they notify him whether they favour a conference of States Parties\nfor the purpose of considering and voting upon the proposals. In the event that\nat least one third of the States Parties favours such a conference, the\nSecretary-General shall convene the conference under the auspices of the\nUnited Nations. Any amendment adopted by a majority of the States Parties\npresent and voting at the conference shall be submitted to the General Assembly\nof the United Nations for approval.\n2.\nAmendments shall come into force when they have been approved by\nthe General Assembly of the United Nations and accepted by a two-thirds\n\nInternational Covenant on Civil and Political Rights  Schedule 2\n\nAustralian Human Rights Commission Act 1986\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\nmajority of the States Parties to the present Covenant in accordance with their\nrespective constitutional processes.\n3.\nWhen amendments come into force, they shall be binding on those\nStates Parties which have accepted them, other States Parties still being bound\nby the provisions of the present Covenant and any earlier amendment which\nthey have accepted.",
      "skos:definition": "Any State Party to the present Covenant may propose an amendment\nand file it with the Secretary-General of the United Nations. The\nSecretary-General of the United Nations shall thereupon communicate any\nproposed amendments to the States Parties to the present Covenant with a\nrequest that they notify him whether they favour a conference of States Parties\nfor the purpose of considering and voting upon the proposals. In the event that\nat least one third of the States Parties favours such a conference, the\nSecretary-General shall convene the conference under the auspices of the\nUnited Nations. Any amendment adopted by a majority of the States Parties\npresent and voting at the conference shall be submitted to the General Assembly\nof the United Nations for approval.\n2.\nAmendments shall come into force when they have been approved by\nthe General Assembly of the United Nations and accepted by a two-thirds\n\nInternational Covenant on Civil and Political Rights  Schedule 2\n\nAustralian Human Rights Commission Act 1986\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\nmajority of the States Parties to the present Covenant in accordance with their\nrespective constitutional processes.\n3.\nWhen amendments come into force, they shall be binding on those\nStates Parties which have accepted them, other States Parties still being bound\nby the provisions of the present Covenant and any earlier amendment which\nthey have accepted.",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-human-rights-commission-act-1986-article-51-norm"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "f05b84552395bdb33576a7e885a4ec60f05ffea2f83201f2be238f09f45a0318",
      "skos:note": "Proposal for amendment of Covenant"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-human-rights-commission-act-1986#article-51",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "51 1.",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 150,
      "values:originalText": "Any State Party to the present Covenant may propose an amendment\nand file it with the Secretary-General of the United Nations. The\nSecretary-General of the United Nations shall thereupon communicate any\nproposed amendments to the States Parties to the present Covenant with a\nrequest that they notify him whether they favour a conference of States Parties\nfor the purpose of considering and voting upon the proposals. In the event that\nat least one third of the States Parties favours such a conference, the\nSecretary-General shall convene the conference under the auspices of the\nUnited Nations. Any amendment adopted by a majority of the States Parties\npresent and voting at the conference shall be submitted to the General Assembly\nof the United Nations for approval.\n2.\nAmendments shall come into force when they have been approved by\nthe General Assembly of the United Nations and accepted by a two-thirds\n\nInternational Covenant on Civil and Political Rights  Schedule 2\n\nAustralian Human Rights Commission Act 1986\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\nmajority of the States Parties to the present Covenant in accordance with their\nrespective constitutional processes.\n3.\nWhen amendments come into force, they shall be binding on those\nStates Parties which have accepted them, other States Parties still being bound\nby the provisions of the present Covenant and any earlier amendment which\nthey have accepted."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-human-rights-commission-act-1986-article-51-norm",
